window.__DIYDB__=window.__DIYDB__||{data:{}};window.__DIYDB__.data["gladiator"]=[{"id":"jt-front-bumper","title":"Steel Front Bumper for the Gladiator JT","vehicle":"gladiator","category":"armor","type":"part","difficulty":3,"time_hours":{"low":2,"high":4},"cost_usd":{"low":549,"high":1599},"tools_required":["socket set","torque wrench","helping hands or floor jack for weight support","wire harness connectors if sensors present"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching","electrical connectors"],"build_tiers":["mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2020-2024"],"tags":["armor","bumper","winch-ready","protection"],"parts":[{"name":"Rugged Ridge Spartan Front Bumper for JT","vendor":"Rugged Ridge","price_estimate_usd":549},{"name":"Warn Ascent Front Bumper for Gladiator JT","vendor":"Warn","price_estimate_usd":1099},{"name":"Fab Four Stubby Front Bumper for JT","vendor":"Fab Four","price_estimate_usd":1449},{"name":"Smittybilt XRC Front Bumper for Gladiator JT","vendor":"Smittybilt","price_estimate_usd":599}],"citations":[{"title":"Warn Ascent JT Bumper Install","url":"https://www.warn.com/accessories/bumpers/jeep-gladiator"},{"title":"Rugged Ridge Spartan JT Bumper Product Page","url":"https://www.ruggedridge.com/jeep-gladiator-jt-bumpers"}],"alternatives":["jt-winch","jt-sliders"],"summary":"The JT shares its front clip geometry with the JL Wrangler \u2014 which means the bumper selection is wide \u2014 but sensor compatibility and winch integration vary enough by trim that it pays to check before you order.","description":"The Gladiator JT's front end is mechanically similar to the JL Wrangler up to the firewall, and most JL bumpers fit the JT front with no modification or minor bracket adjustments. That's useful news because it nearly doubles the selection available to JT owners compared to JT-only fitments. Some aftermarket manufacturers note their JL bumpers as \"fits JT with adapter\" \u2014 read those listings carefully, because the adapter is sometimes just a relocation bracket for a fog light or sensor, and sometimes it's a more involved mounting change.\n\nSensor compatibility is the variable to check by trim. Sport and Sport S trims typically lack front parking sensors and radar-based adaptive cruise, making bumper selection straightforward. Overland, Rubicon, and some Mojave trims include front sensors embedded in the factory bumper that need to transfer to or be deleted with the aftermarket replacement. Most quality bumpers include sensor ports or cutouts \u2014 verify that the specific bumper you're ordering accommodates your trim's sensor layout before purchasing.\n\nThe Rugged Ridge Spartan ($549) is the right answer for owners who want the look and basic protection without winch integration \u2014 it's steel, it mounts solid, and it won't break the budget. The Warn Ascent ($1,099) is the step up if you're mounting a winch: the fairlead is integrated cleanly, the recovery points are rated, and Warn's bumper build quality is consistent. Fab Four's Stubby ($1,449) goes the other direction \u2014 a short front bumper that maximizes approach angle at the cost of full-width protection. It's the choice for technical rock crawling where approach angle matters more than bumper coverage.","steps":["Disconnect the negative battery terminal before starting \u2014 front bumper removal involves disconnecting factory tow hook sensors and fog light wiring on most trims.","Remove the factory bumper end caps and skid panel if present.","Locate and disconnect any factory sensor connectors in the bumper \u2014 photograph the routing before unplugging.","Remove the factory bumper mounting bolts (typically 4\u20136 bolts into the frame rails). The factory bumper is heavy \u2014 have a second person or a floor jack ready.","Mount the new bumper to the frame using the supplied hardware. Hand-thread all bolts before torquing any.","Route and reconnect fog lights and sensors per bumper instructions. Some bumpers include pass-through wiring harnesses; others require you to extend the factory harness.","Torque bumper mounting bolts to spec (typically 45\u201365 ft-lbs depending on kit).","Reconnect the battery and verify all lights and sensors function correctly."],"pros":["Wide selection due to JL/JT front clip compatibility","Winch-ready designs integrate recovery hardware without a separate winch plate","Steel construction protects against trail and parking lot contact alike","Improved approach angle with stubby or mid-width designs"],"cons":["Sensor compatibility must be verified by trim \u2014 Sport and Overland have different sensor layouts","Heavier than factory bumper \u2014 expect 60\u2013120 lbs added to the front end","Some JL bumpers listed as \"JT compatible\" require adapter brackets not included in base price","Adds cost to airbag system recalibration on some trims if sensor circuits are disrupted"]},{"id":"jt-rear-bumper","title":"Rear Bumper for the Gladiator JT \u2014 Pickup Bed Integration","vehicle":"gladiator","category":"armor","type":"part","difficulty":3,"time_hours":{"low":2,"high":4},"cost_usd":{"low":799,"high":1599},"tools_required":["socket set","torque wrench","pry bar","electrical connectors","floor jack for weight support"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching","electrical connectors","wiring basics"],"build_tiers":["mild_trail","moderate_trail","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2020-2024"],"tags":["armor","bumper","rear","recovery","truck-bed"],"parts":[{"name":"Warn Ascent Rear Bumper for Gladiator JT","vendor":"Warn","price_estimate_usd":1399},{"name":"Addictive Desert Designs Gladiator JT Rear Bumper","vendor":"Addictive Desert Designs","price_estimate_usd":1299},{"name":"Poison Spyder JT Rear Crusher Bumper","vendor":"Poison Spyder Customs","price_estimate_usd":1199}],"citations":[{"title":"Warn Ascent JT Rear Bumper \u2014 Product Overview","url":"https://www.warn.com/accessories/bumpers/jeep-gladiator-rear"},{"title":"Addictive Desert Designs JT Rear Bumper Guide","url":"https://www.addictivedesertdesigns.com/gladiator"}],"alternatives":["jt-front-bumper","jt-sliders"],"summary":"The JT's rear bumper is completely different from any Wrangler rear \u2014 the pickup bed floor and tailgate change every mounting dimension, and a Wrangler rear bumper will not fit.","description":"This is the point where Gladiator owners most often get burned by shopping the wrong vehicle. The JL Wrangler's rear bumper is a simple crossmember-and-tube unit that clears the cargo area. The JT's rear bumper has to account for the pickup bed floor sitting several inches lower than a Wrangler's tub, the tailgate's lower pivot point, and the bed's frame rails extending rearward. JT-specific rear bumpers are built around those dimensions \u2014 they're a different part designed from scratch. If a listing doesn't say JT, it won't fit.\n\nBeyond fitment, the JT's rear also involves two practical questions most buyers don't think about until they're partway through the install: hitch receiver placement and backup camera retention. Most JT owners want to keep the hitch receiver for towing \u2014 the JT is rated to tow up to 7,650 lbs depending on powertrain \u2014 and not all aftermarket rear bumpers include a receiver tube or position it at the same height as the factory unit. Verify hitch integration before ordering. Backup camera retention is similarly variable: some bumpers include a camera relocation bracket; others require you to delete the camera or source an aftermarket mount.\n\nThe Warn Ascent JT rear ($1,399) handles both issues cleanly \u2014 it includes a full-size 2\" receiver and a camera port in the correct location. Addictive Desert Designs' JT rear ($1,299) is a more aggressive-looking steel unit popular on trail-focused builds, with shackle mounts and recovery points rated for recovery loads. Poison Spyder's JT Crusher ($1,199) is the budget leader in the heavy-steel category, with a functional if less refined finish.","steps":["Disconnect the negative battery terminal \u2014 the backup camera circuit runs through the rear bumper area.","Locate and disconnect the backup camera connector and any trailer wiring connected to the factory bumper.","Remove the factory step pad (if equipped) and the factory tow receiver or D-ring mounts.","Unbolt the factory rear bumper from the frame rails \u2014 typically 6\u20138 bolts. Have a helper or use a floor jack under the bumper; factory rear bumpers are heavier than they look.","Position the new bumper and hand-thread all mounting hardware before tightening anything.","Reconnect backup camera using the provided bracket or relocation mount \u2014 test camera function before finalizing.","Route trailer wiring through the new bumper's wiring pass-through if equipped.","Torque all bumper mounting bolts to spec (kit-specific; typically 45\u201365 ft-lbs).","Reinstall hitch receiver if it's a separate component."],"pros":["Replaces the factory plastic lower valance with steel protection at the most vulnerable departure-angle impact point","JT-specific designs maintain hitch receiver position and backup camera function","Recovery-point integration provides rated anchor points not present on the factory bumper","Improves departure angle on lifted JTs by pulling the lower bumper edge up and in"],"cons":["No JL/Wrangler crossover \u2014 this is a JT-only part; selection is narrower than the front bumper market","High price tier relative to rear bumpers on most other platforms","Backup camera relocation or deletion required on most installs","Heavy steel adds weight directly behind the rear axle \u2014 factor into towing tongue weight calculations"]},{"id":"jt-skid-plates","title":"Skid Plate Kit for the Gladiator JT","vehicle":"gladiator","category":"armor","type":"kit","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":2,"high":4},"cost_usd":{"low":399,"high":799},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","socket set","torque wrench"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching","under-vehicle access"],"build_tiers":["mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler"],"year_compatibility":["2020-2024"],"tags":["armor","skid-plate","protection","underbody"],"parts":[{"name":"Teraflex JT Skid Plate Kit","vendor":"Teraflex","price_estimate_usd":499},{"name":"Rock Hard 4x4 JT Gladiator Skid Plate System","vendor":"Rock Hard 4x4","price_estimate_usd":699},{"name":"Rubicon Express JT Skid Plate Kit","vendor":"Rubicon Express","price_estimate_usd":449}],"citations":[{"title":"Teraflex Gladiator JT Skid Plate Install","url":"https://www.teraflex.com/products/gladiator-skid-plates"},{"title":"Rock Hard 4x4 JT Skid System Overview","url":"https://www.rockhard4x4.com/jeep-gladiator-jt-skid-plates"}],"alternatives":["jt-sliders","jt-front-bumper"],"summary":"The Gladiator JT's longer 137\" wheelbase means more underbody exposure per trail obstacle \u2014 skid plates that fit the JL won't cover the full length of the JT, and buying a JL set is money wasted.","description":"Stock JT underbody protection is minimal outside of a factory transfer case skid on Rubicon trims. The oil pan, transmission, fuel tank, and the stretch of unprotected frame between those components are all exposed to rock strikes on any trail with ledges or embedded rock. For a truck with the JT's capability envelope \u2014 and the market it attracts, which skews toward owners who actually use the truck \u2014 running without skids is a one-trail mistake.\n\nThe coverage priority on the JT is: (1) engine/oil pan, (2) transmission, (3) transfer case, (4) fuel tank. A kit that addresses all four gives you full protection from bumper to bumper on the underside. The JT's longer wheelbase means the fuel tank sits further back than on the JL, so JT-specific skids extend coverage rearward in a way JL skids simply can't. Teraflex's JT kit ($499) covers the first three in one purchase; the Rock Hard 4x4 system ($699) extends full coverage to the fuel tank and adds heavier plate gauge (3/16\" vs. 1/8\") for a rock-crawling-focused build. Rubicon Express's $449 kit is the value option and covers the essential surfaces without the upgrade-gauge material.\n\nPlate gauge matters when you're shopping. 1/8\" (11 gauge) steel is adequate for trail skimming and ledge glances; 3/16\" is the right call for sustained rock contact in a crawling context. Aluminum skid plates exist in this price category too \u2014 they're significantly lighter but dent rather than deflect on hard hits, which can transfer impact energy to the component they're protecting. Steel is the correct choice for JTs going on rock.","steps":["Raise the vehicle and support on jack stands under the frame at all four corners \u2014 you need clearance to work under the full length of the truck.","Locate the factory mounting points on the frame per the kit instructions. Most JT skid systems use existing threaded frame holes; some require supplied nut plates slid into the frame channel.","Start with the forward engine/transmission skid: position the plate, insert all bolts hand-tight, then torque in a cross pattern.","Install the transfer case skid next if supplied separately \u2014 overlap the rearmost edge of the transmission skid for continuous coverage.","Install the fuel tank skid last \u2014 verify it doesn't contact the fuel tank directly at any point before tightening.","Inspect all mounting bolts after first trail use and re-torque as needed."],"pros":["Protects the most vulnerable underbody components from rock strikes, ledges, and road debris","JT-specific fitment covers the full wheelbase length \u2014 no coverage gaps","Steel construction deflects impacts rather than transmitting them to the component","Bolt-on install accessible to any home mechanic with basic tools"],"cons":["JL skid plates will not cover the JT's full length \u2014 verify JT fitment before ordering","Adds 30\u201370 lbs of underbody weight depending on gauge and coverage","Access to oil drain plug and transmission service points may require removing a skid panel \u2014 note this before your first oil change","Quality steel plates at this price tier have basic powder coat that chips; touch up annually"]},{"id":"jt-sliders","title":"Rock Sliders for the Gladiator JT","vehicle":"gladiator","category":"armor","type":"part","difficulty":3,"time_hours":{"low":3,"high":5},"cost_usd":{"low":449,"high":799},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","socket set","torque wrench","drill","wire brush"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching","drilling into body/frame"],"build_tiers":["mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler"],"year_compatibility":["2020-2024"],"tags":["armor","sliders","protection","rock-rail"],"parts":[{"name":"Poison Spyder Rock Sliders for Gladiator JT","vendor":"Poison Spyder Customs","price_estimate_usd":699},{"name":"Rock Hard 4x4 JT Gladiator Rock Rails","vendor":"Rock Hard 4x4","price_estimate_usd":449},{"name":"LOD Destroyer JT Rock Sliders","vendor":"LOD Offroad","price_estimate_usd":699},{"name":"Smittybilt XRC Rock Sliders for JT","vendor":"Smittybilt","price_estimate_usd":499}],"citations":[{"title":"Poison Spyder JT Rock Slider Install Guide","url":"https://www.poisonspyder.com/gladiator-jt-sliders"},{"title":"Rock Hard 4x4 JT Gladiator Rock Rail Product Page","url":"https://www.rockhard4x4.com/jeep-gladiator-jt"}],"alternatives":["jt-front-bumper","jt-skid-plates"],"summary":"Rock sliders protect the JT's rocker panels when the trail gets narrow and you're trading paint with the terrain \u2014 and the Gladiator's longer wheelbase means those rockers are exposed across more of the truck's length than on a Wrangler.","description":"The JT Gladiator is 19\" longer than the JL Unlimited, and that extra length shows up in the rocker panel exposure on the trail. A JL slider won't cover a JT's rockers \u2014 this is not a cross-compatible part. When you're sourcing sliders for the JT, verify the listing says JT explicitly and check the coverage dimensions. Quality JT sliders should run the full length of the cab door openings, with enough tube diameter to take side contact without deforming into the rocker.\n\nThe core decision is weld-on vs. bolt-on. Weld-on sliders (like the Poison Spyder JT at $699 and the LOD Destroyer at $699) mount directly to the frame with stronger, lower-profile attachment \u2014 they don't depend on bolt holes in factory pinch-weld flanges. They're the right choice for a dedicated trail truck. Bolt-on sliders (Rock Hard 4x4 at $449, Smittybilt XRC at $499) use existing frame holes or require drilling into the body/frame at specific points. The install is accessible without a welder, but bolt-on designs have more flex under hard lateral contact. That doesn't mean they fail \u2014 the Rock Hard 4x4 units are well-regarded bolt-on sliders that have taken real abuse \u2014 but weld-on is structurally superior when you're doing legitimate rock work.\n\nSmittybilt's XRC JT sliders at $499 are the budget-to-quality inflection point: bolt-on install, step functionality, and enough steel tube to handle moderate trail abuse. If you're building an overland-focused JT that occasionally sees rocky trails rather than a dedicated rock crawler, the Smittybilt units are honest value. Poison Spyder and LOD are the right call for a wheeling-first build where the sliders will get used hard.","steps":["Clean and degrease the frame rail area along the rocker panels where sliders will mount.","For bolt-on kits: locate the factory mounting points per kit instructions; drill any additional holes required. Use a center punch to prevent drill drift.","For weld-on kits: position sliders using clamps and verify fitment before any welding. Tack-weld first, check alignment, then complete the weld.","Apply the supplied hardware (bolt-on kits): hand-thread all bolts before torquing any, then torque to spec in a cross pattern.","Inspect the slider length against the door sill \u2014 sliders should extend from the front door hinge area to at least the rear door hinge on the driver and passenger sides.","Coat any raw or bare metal areas with rattle-can flat black or bedliner to prevent rust."],"pros":["Protects rocker panels from the most common trail damage source: rock contact on side slopes","Doubles as a step for cab entry on lifted JTs","JT-specific fitment covers the full longer wheelbase cab","Weld-on options are among the strongest armor dollar-per-dollar available"],"cons":["JL sliders will not fit \u2014 verify JT fitment before ordering","Weld-on install requires a welder or a fabrication shop; adds $150\u2013$300 to install cost","Adds 30\u201360 lbs per side \u2014 minor but worth noting on a truck you're weight-managing","Powder coat chips on rocks; touch-up paint or bedliner recoat is ongoing maintenance"]},{"id":"jt-rear-locker-trail-use","title":"Trail-using the Rubicon's rear locker only","vehicle":"gladiator","category":"axles","subcategory":"lockers","type":"how_to","difficulty":1,"time_hours":{"low":0.0,"high":0.0},"cost_usd":{"low":0,"high":0},"tools_required":[],"skills_required":["trail driving awareness","locker operation"],"build_tiers":["mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler"],"year_compatibility":["2020-present"],"tags":["locker","rear locker","front locker","rubicon","trail driving","AdaptTrac","electronic locker"],"parts":[],"citations":[{"title":"Jeep Gladiator Rubicon owner's manual \u2014 locker operation","url":"https://www.jeep.com/gladiator"}],"alternatives":[],"summary":"Engaging only the rear locker is often the right call on trail \u2014 it provides the traction benefit with more steering ability than running front and rear locked together. Knowing when to use what is more valuable than knowing how to engage everything at once.","description":"The Rubicon runs electronic lockers in both the front Dana 44 and the rear Dana 44. These are selectable differential locks \u2014 when engaged, both wheels on that axle are driven at the same speed regardless of traction conditions. The lockers can be engaged independently via the instrument cluster controls.\n\nThe system operates in 4L (low range) by default for full locker engagement. The rear locker can be engaged in 4H on some terrain conditions.\n\n**Moderate trail with steering demands:** When you need traction but also need to steer precisely \u2014 navigating around obstacles, threading a line through loose rock \u2014 rear-only lock provides drive traction through the rear axle while leaving the front axle free to respond to steering inputs. Locking the front as well reduces steering feel and creates understeer on tight turns.\n\n**Mud and loose terrain:** In deep mud or loose dirt, the rear locker keeps the rear wheels driving together without spinning down. The front axle in open-diff mode can still find traction and contribute, while the rear pushes. This is often enough to get through without front lock.\n\n**Highway-adjacent situations:** If you find yourself in a difficult low-speed situation on or near pavement (a steep gravel driveway, a muddy turnaround), rear-only lock in 4H can provide the traction you need without forcing the front diff to fight the drivetrain binding that full lock creates on hard surfaces.\n\nAdd the front locker when rear-only isn't enough \u2014 typically:\n\n- **Full-on rock crawling:** Both wheels on both axles need to drive together when you're on slick rock or technical terrain where one wheel can easily lift\n- **Deep water crossings:** Maximum drive traction ensures you push through without getting stuck mid-crossing\n- **Severely uneven terrain:** When you're at the limit of articulation and one front wheel is off the ground\n\nWith both lockers engaged, the steering fights back. The truck wants to go straight; turning requires more input and creates axle binding on tight turns. On slick rock this binding is managed by terrain flex. On hard-pack or pavement, binding is uncomfortable and puts stress on the drivetrain.\n\nDrive full-locked at low speed, on terrain that justifies it, and unlock before returning to any hard surface.\n\nFor reference on trail approach:\n\n| Axle Setup | Description | Trail Use |\n|---|---|---|\n| Open differential | Torque goes to the wheel with least resistance \u2014 worst case, one spinning wheel | Fine for mild trail |\n| Limited-slip (LSD/Trac-Lok) | Biases torque toward the wheel with more traction \u2014 better than open | Good for moderate terrain |\n| Locked differential | Both wheels driven at equal speed regardless of traction | Maximum traction, reduced steering |\n\nThe Rubicon's electronic lockers are true lockers \u2014 not limited-slip. Engage them for terrain that demands it, not as a default trail setting.","steps":["Select 4L for technical terrain (ensures proper engagement range)","Engage rear locker via the instrument cluster control \u2014 wait for the indicator light to confirm lock","Drive the section \u2014 assess whether rear-only is sufficient","If more traction is needed, engage front locker as well","After clearing the difficult section, disengage front locker first, then rear if appropriate","Return to 4H or 2H before returning to any hard surface or higher-speed driving"],"pros":["Rear-only lock is a useful middle-ground state \u2014 more traction than open, better steering than full-locked","Electronic engagement is fast and reliable","Independent front/rear control lets you match the locker setup to the terrain demand"],"cons":["Driving locked on pavement or hard surfaces creates drivetrain binding and wear \u2014 unlock before returning to road","Rear locker on high-traction surfaces with hard acceleration can cause axle wrap \u2014 be smooth with the throttle","Some owners lock everything by default and miss the value of rear-only for moderate terrain"]},{"id":"frame-bend-towing-recall","title":"Gladiator frame bend recall and tongue weight limits","vehicle":"gladiator","category":"body","subcategory":"frame integrity","type":"diagnostic","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":1.0,"high":2.0},"cost_usd":{"low":0,"high":0},"tools_required":["flashlight","straightedge or level (for visual inspection)"],"skills_required":["visual inspection"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2020-2022"],"tags":["frame","recall","towing","tongue weight","NHTSA","stellantis","structural"],"parts":[],"citations":[{"title":"NHTSA Recall 21V-127 \u2014 Gladiator Frame","url":"https://www.nhtsa.gov/recalls"},{"title":"Stellantis Service Action \u2014 Gladiator Frame Investigation","url":"https://www.mopar.com/en-us/care/recalls.html"}],"alternatives":[],"summary":"Early Gladiators (primarily 2020\u20132021 model years) experienced frame bends and cracks linked to towing with excessive tongue weight. Stellantis investigated and issued a recall covering affected vehicles. If you own an early JT and tow, verify the recall status and practice tongue weight discipline \u2014 the tow rating is real, but the tongue weight limit is where people get into trouble.","description":"Stellantis received reports of Gladiator frames showing deformation \u2014 bending and cracking \u2014 primarily at the rear frame rails behind the cab, near the hitch receiver mounting area. The failures were associated with towing scenarios where tongue weight exceeded the recommended limit.\n\nThe Gladiator frame is a pickup-truck ladder frame, but the JT's wheelbase is shorter than most truck platforms. The shorter rear overhang concentrates tongue weight stress at a tighter section of the frame. When tongue weight runs high \u2014 especially with heavier trailers or loads positioned too far back in the trailer \u2014 that stress can exceed what the frame tolerates.\n\nThe NHTSA investigation (NHTSA ID PE21-002) covered early production Gladiators. Stellantis conducted an Engineering Analysis and issued a service action. Check the NHTSA recall database with your VIN to confirm whether your truck is affected and whether the service action has been completed.\n\nTotal tow rating gets the headlines. Tongue weight is where the actual failures happened.\n\nThe Gladiator's tongue weight limit is 10% of trailer weight, with a max that varies by configuration. On a 5,000 lb trailer, that means 500 lb maximum tongue weight. On a 7,650 lb trailer (Sport with Max Tow), that's 765 lb.\n\n**The common mistake:** People load trailers and then add gear at the rear of the trailer to \"balance\" it. This pushes weight toward the tongue and over the limit. The trailer should be loaded with 60% of its weight ahead of the axle \u2014 this is how tongue weight stays in the 10\u201312% range.\n\nA tongue weight scale is a $30\u2013$60 tool that attaches to the ball mount and lets you read tongue weight directly before you put the truck under load. If you tow regularly, this is worth having.\n\nIf you can't weigh it, use the 60/40 rule: place the heavier items in the front half of the trailer (ahead of the axle), lighter items in the rear. Run the numbers: trailer weight \u00d7 0.10 = maximum tongue weight in lb.\n\nOn early Gladiators, inspect the rear frame rails behind the cab:\n\n- Look for cracking or deformation along the top and bottom flanges of the frame rail\n- Check the area around the receiver hitch mounting bolts \u2014 look for elongated bolt holes or cracking at the attachment points\n- Look at the crossmember that ties the rear frame rails together \u2014 deformation here is a flag\n- Any visible bend in the frame rail is serious and warrants a shop visit before towing again\n\nA frame that has bent does not bend back. If you find deformation, stop towing until the truck is inspected by a qualified shop and the frame is evaluated.","steps":["Go to nhtsa.gov/recalls and enter your VIN","Check for any open recalls or service actions on the Gladiator frame","If your truck is covered by the recall and the service action hasn't been completed, contact a Jeep/Stellantis dealer \u2014 this is warranty-covered work","Inspect the rear frame rails with a flashlight","If you currently tow, calculate your tongue weight (trailer weight \u00d7 0.10) and compare it to your actual tongue weight","Adjust trailer loading if tongue weight is above 10%"],"pros":["The recall and investigation resulted in a clear service path for affected trucks","Most early trucks have had the service action completed through dealer network","Practicing tongue weight discipline prevents the failure mode entirely"],"cons":["Frame damage from over-tongue-weight towing is not covered under recall if the vehicle was loaded beyond spec","A bent frame is a serious structural issue \u2014 it's not a cosmetic repair","Used-market JTs may not have recall work completed \u2014 always verify VIN before purchase"]},{"id":"jt-trim-buyers-guide","title":"Gladiator trim guide: Sport vs Willys vs Rubicon vs Mojave","vehicle":"gladiator","category":"build_recipe","subcategory":"trim comparison","type":"reference","difficulty":1,"time_hours":{"low":0.5,"high":1.0},"cost_usd":{"low":0,"high":0},"tools_required":[],"skills_required":["research","budget planning"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2020-present"],"tags":["trim","buyers guide","rubicon","mojave","willys","sport","comparison"],"parts":[],"citations":[{"title":"Jeep Gladiator Specs \u2014 FCA Media","url":"https://www.media.stellantis.com/us-en/jeep/press-releases"},{"title":"Gladiator Mojave Desert-Rated information","url":"https://www.jeep.com/gladiator"}],"alternatives":[],"summary":"The Rubicon is the trail rig. The Mojave is the desert runner. The Sport and Willys are clean starting points for a custom build. Pick based on what the truck will actually do, not the badge.","description":"The Gladiator lineup splits along two fundamentally different axes: rock-crawling capability (Rubicon) and high-speed desert performance (Mojave). The Sport and Willys sit below both \u2014 they're capable trucks with the same solid-axle bones, but without the factory lockers, sway-bar disconnect, and upgraded suspension you'd need to buy anyway if you go that direction.\n\nBefore comparing trims, answer this: do you want to build the truck yourself, or do you want factory capability out of the gate? If you're building, start with Sport or Willys and save the price difference. If you want to drive it off the lot and onto technical terrain the same weekend, the Rubicon makes the math work.\n\nThe entry point. Solid Dana 44 axles front and rear \u2014 this is meaningful. The Gladiator doesn't have a weak axle option; the base truck runs the same 44s as the Rubicon, just without lockers or the electronic disconnect. The Sport runs 32-inch tires (LT255/75R17) and a 3.73 axle ratio. The suspension is the base coil-and-leaf setup \u2014 adequate for mild trail and daily driving, not competitive on technical terrain.\n\n**Sport S** adds convenience and comfort features (8.4-inch Uconnect, power windows, keyless entry). No mechanical upgrades. If you're building from the bottom up, Sport S is the trim to start with \u2014 it has enough creature comforts to live with daily without paying for Willys or Overland features you'll discard.\n\nThe value tier for people who actually trail-drive. The Willys adds rock rails, a limited-slip rear differential (in place of an open diff), 32-inch all-terrain tires, and a few suspension upgrades. The LSD is meaningful \u2014 it's not a locker, but it maintains traction better than an open diff under load. The Willys also gets a heavy-duty Dana 44 rear axle with a Trac-Lok LSD, which is a real improvement.\n\nIf you want to start trail-driving immediately without modification and don't want to pay for the Rubicon, the Willys is the honest buy. It's not as capable on rocks \u2014 no front locker, no sway-bar disconnect \u2014 but it's a better truck than the Sport for actual use.\n\nBuilt for technical terrain. The Rubicon gets what matters for rock crawling: electronic front and rear lockers (Dana 44 AdaptTrac), front sway-bar electric disconnect, 33-inch mud-terrain tires (LT285/70R17), and 4.10 axle gears. It also gets rock rails, skid plates (gas tank, transfer case, front axle), and the Rock-Trac NV241 transfer case with a 4:1 low range.\n\nThe sway-bar disconnect is worth understanding: on the trail, disconnecting the front sway bar allows the axles to articulate freely, dramatically increasing wheel travel. The Rubicon can do this at the push of a button. Adding this to a Sport after purchase is expensive and complex.\n\n**The honest catch:** The Rubicon's 1,200 lb payload rating is the lowest in the lineup. A Rubicon with two adults, a full tank, and a bed-mounted camper shell is already close to its limit. If you plan to haul cargo or tow with passengers on board, this matters.\n\nCompletely different use case. The Mojave is the only production Jeep to be rated Desert-Rated (as opposed to Trail-Rated). It's built for high-speed desert running, not rock crawling.\n\nThe Mojave-specific hardware: Fox 2.5-inch Internal Bypass shocks (front and rear), a hydraulic jounce bumper system in the front, 17-inch beadlock-capable wheels, a 2-inch front lift over Sport, and 33-inch Falken Wildpeak A/T tires. The front suspension travel is substantially longer than any other Gladiator trim.\n\n**What the Mojave doesn't have:** a front locker, a front sway-bar disconnect. The desert-running calibration trades technical rock capability for speed and comfort over rough terrain. If you're planning runs in the desert Southwest \u2014 Sonoran Desert, Baja-style terrain \u2014 the Mojave is the right tool. If you're going to Moab or the Rubicon Trail, buy the Rubicon.\n\nThe comfort-forward trim. Heated leather seats, 8.4-inch Uconnect, premium audio, larger fuel tank (24 gallons vs 17.5 on the Sport). Mechanically it sits between the Willys and Rubicon \u2014 it gets the 3.73 ratio and doesn't have lockers or sway-bar disconnect. Overland buyers are typically using the truck as a daily driver with occasional light-trail use. That's a fine use case; just don't confuse it with capability.\n\nThe top-trim Rubicon variant, added in the 2023 model year. Upgraded interior, steel bumpers, LED lighting, and additional content. The mechanical package is the same as the standard Rubicon. If the standard Rubicon fits the budget, the X is mostly comfort and appearance upgrades.\n\n- **For rock crawling:** Rubicon. No trail tier comes close for factory capability.\n- **For high-speed desert terrain:** Mojave, no competition.\n- **For building from scratch:** Sport or Willys. Save the trim premium and spend it on the parts that actually matter for your use case.\n- **For daily driving with light trail use:** Overland or Willys Sport.","steps":["Determine your primary terrain (rocks, desert, mixed)","Set a realistic payload budget \u2014 subtract passengers, gear, and any camper weight from your trim's rating","Decide how much you want the factory to have done versus doing it yourself","Price out the gap between a lower trim + your target upgrades vs. buying the Rubicon/Mojave outright"],"pros":["Every Gladiator gets solid Dana 44 axles \u2014 the platform is strong regardless of trim","The Rubicon is genuinely capable from the factory; it doesn't need $5,000 in mods to get on trail","The Mojave's Fox 2.5 internal bypass shocks are a legitimate performance item; they can't be replicated cheaply"],"cons":["The Rubicon's 1,200 lb payload is tight for a work truck or camper setup","The Mojave has no front locker \u2014 it cannot keep up with a Rubicon on technical terrain","The price gap between Sport and Rubicon is significant; do the math before assuming the Rubicon is always the answer"]},{"id":"payload-towing-reality","title":"Gladiator payload and towing: the honest numbers","vehicle":"gladiator","category":"build_recipe","subcategory":"payload and towing","type":"reference","difficulty":1,"time_hours":{"low":0.5,"high":1.0},"cost_usd":{"low":0,"high":0},"tools_required":[],"skills_required":["research"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2020-present"],"tags":["payload","towing","max tow","rubicon","overland","weight"],"parts":[],"citations":[{"title":"Jeep Gladiator Owner's Manual \u2014 Payload and Towing sections","url":"https://www.jeep.com/gladiator"}],"alternatives":[],"summary":"The Gladiator's payload and tow ratings vary significantly by trim. A Rubicon's 1,200 lb payload disappears fast. The 7,650 lb tow rating is real, but it requires the Max Tow Package on a Sport-level trim \u2014 not the Rubicon.","description":"| Trim | Payload (approx.) | Max Tow (approx.) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Sport with Max Tow | 1,710 lb | 7,650 lb |\n| Sport S | 1,600 lb | 4,000 lb |\n| Willys | 1,540 lb | 4,000 lb |\n| Overland | 1,490 lb | 6,000 lb |\n| Rubicon | 1,200 lb | 5,000 lb |\n| Mojave | 1,350 lb | 5,000 lb |\n\nThese are the factory maximums \u2014 the number stamped on the door jamb sticker for your specific build. The sticker number is the number that matters. It accounts for your exact trim, options, and factory-installed equipment. The published spec sheet gives a range; your truck's sticker gives the actual number.\n\nThe Rubicon is heavier than the base trims. Lockers, skid plates, rock rails, beefier axle hardware, the NV241 Rock-Trac transfer case, the sway-bar disconnect actuator \u2014 all of it adds curb weight. The factory sets payload capacity as GVWR minus curb weight, so a heavier truck has less available payload even if the GVWR is the same. The Rubicon's additional equipment is what cuts its payload rating to 1,200 lb.\n\nThe 7,650 lb tow rating is achievable only on the Sport and Sport S with the Max Tow Package. The package includes a heavy-duty alternator, trailer brake controller, upgraded cooling, Class IV trailer hitch, four-pin and seven-pin electrical connectors, and a 3.73 axle ratio (held to that ratio; if you spec a different ratio, you lose the rating).\n\nThe Rubicon cannot reach 7,650 lb. Its platform is Trail-Rated, not Max Tow rated, and its heavier curb weight works against towing capacity.\n\nA Rubicon towing a loaded camp trailer with two adults in the cab:\n\n- Two adults: ~340 lb\n- Full 17.5-gallon fuel tank: ~115 lb\n- Loaded bed (recovery gear, camp gear): ~300 lb\n- That's 755 lb used before the trailer hitch is connected.\n\nWith 1,200 lb total capacity, that leaves 445 lb for tongue weight and anything else in the bed. A medium-sized camp trailer like a small teardrop or a pop-up can easily put 400\u2013500 lb on the tongue. You're at the limit before the trailer's loaded.\n\nOverland buyers in camp trailers face this calculation constantly. The Gladiator is a shorter-wheelbase platform than a full-size truck. It was designed for trail access, not towing heavy loads. The platform is honest about that.\n\nRegardless of tow rating, the Gladiator has a specific tongue weight limit: 10% of trailer weight, up to a maximum. The frame bend issues documented in early production Gladiators were specifically linked to tongue weight exceeding limits \u2014 not overall tow weight. See the separate frame bend recall guide for details.\n\nThe takeaway: even if you're well within tow rating, managing tongue weight to stay at 10\u201312% of trailer weight is important on the JT. The platform's pickup-truck framing is shorter than a full-size and sees different stress concentrations when tongue weight is high.","steps":["Find your truck's door jamb sticker \u2014 use the actual payload number, not the spec sheet","Weigh your real-world cargo: passengers, fuel, gear, bed items","Subtract from your payload rating \u2014 what's left is available for tongue weight","If that number is under 400 lb, your current load-out isn't compatible with a trailer","Use a tongue weight scale (or weigh at a truck stop) before committing to a trailer purchase"],"pros":["The Sport with Max Tow is a genuinely capable tow vehicle for mid-size trailers","All trims can tow something useful \u2014 even the Rubicon can pull 5,000 lb","The solid-axle rear means no IFS flex under tongue weight load"],"cons":["The Rubicon's 1,200 lb payload is easy to exceed in real-world camp and trail use","The best tow rating requires a non-Rubicon trim \u2014 trail buyers and tow buyers are often looking at different trucks","The JT's shorter wheelbase compared to full-size trucks affects stability at highway tow speeds \u2014 drive accordingly"]},{"id":"jt-locker-rear","title":"Rear Locker for the Gladiator JT \u2014 Dana 44 Options","vehicle":"gladiator","category":"drivetrain","type":"part","difficulty":4,"time_hours":{"low":4,"high":6},"cost_usd":{"low":549,"high":999},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","socket set","torque wrench","bearing press or shop press","axle seal driver","dial indicator for backlash","RTV sealant"],"skills_required":["axle work","differential service","precision torquing","bearing and seal installation"],"build_tiers":["moderate_trail","rock_crawler"],"year_compatibility":["2020-2024"],"tags":["locker","dana-44","traction","drivetrain"],"parts":[{"name":"ARB Air Locker for Dana 44 Rear (JT compatible)","vendor":"ARB","price_estimate_usd":849},{"name":"Eaton ELocker for Dana 44 Rear","vendor":"Eaton","price_estimate_usd":649},{"name":"Detroit Truetrac Limited Slip for Dana 44","vendor":"Detroit Locker / Eaton","price_estimate_usd":549}],"citations":[{"title":"ARB Air Locker Dana 44 Install Guide","url":"https://www.arb.com.au/lockers/dana-44"},{"title":"Eaton ELocker Dana 44 Product Page","url":"https://www.eatondriveline.com/elocker"}],"alternatives":["jt-regear"],"summary":"The Gladiator Rubicon already comes with Tru-Lok front and rear lockers \u2014 this guide is for Sport, Willys, Overland, and Mojave owners who want locking traction without stepping into Rubicon pricing.","description":"The rear axle on all non-Rubicon JT trims is a Dana 44 with an open differential. An open diff is fine for pavement and light gravel, but on uneven terrain \u2014 a side slope where one rear tire lifts, or a muddy wash where traction is patchy \u2014 an open diff sends power to the spinning tire instead of the tire with grip. A locker eliminates that. It mechanically (or electronically) connects both rear axle shafts so they turn together regardless of traction difference between the tires.\n\nThe ARB Air Locker ($849) is the performance choice: a pneumatic locker that engages and disengages via air pressure, giving you a true open differential on pavement and a locked rear when you activate it on the trail. ARB Air Lockers require a 12V air compressor \u2014 ARB sells a compatible twin-cylinder compressor kit ($369) that mounts under the hood. The combined cost puts the full ARB setup at roughly $1,200, but the on-demand engagement is the only design that genuinely delivers both behaviors without compromise.\n\nThe Eaton ELocker ($649) uses an electric solenoid to lock the differential on demand. It's cleaner to install than the ARB (no air lines), engages nearly as quickly, and costs less. The primary downside is that solenoid failure (which is uncommon but not unheard of) leaves you with a locked rear until you diagnose and repair it \u2014 the ARB at least fails open. The Detroit Truetrac ($549) is a helical gear limited-slip, not a true locker \u2014 it won't mechanically lock both shafts, but it biases torque to the gripping tire more aggressively than an open diff. It's the choice for owners who want improved traction without any driver activation required and don't need a fully locked differential.","steps":["Drain the rear differential fluid; loosen the cover bolts and pull the cover, catching the remaining fluid in a drain pan.","Remove the rear axle shafts: unbolt the backing plate retainers and slide the shafts out.","Remove the differential carrier: mark the bearing caps before removing (caps are side-specific), unbolt the caps, and carefully remove the carrier from the housing.","Remove the ring gear from the old carrier and install it on the new locker differential. Torque ring gear bolts to spec in a cross pattern \u2014 ring gear bolts are typically 70\u201390 ft-lbs.","Install the new carrier into the housing, checking bearing preload and ring/pinion backlash with a dial indicator. Backlash on Dana 44 should be .006\"\u2013.010\".","Reinstall bearing caps \u2014 torque to spec, position-specific.","For ARB: drill and tap the air fitting port in the diff housing per ARB's instructions. Route the air line through the axle housing to the solenoid.","Reinstall axle shafts and backing plates. Install new axle seals with a seal driver.","Clean the diff cover mating surface, apply RTV, and reinstall cover. Fill with gear oil (75W-140 synthetic recommended; approximately 1.5\u20132 quarts for Dana 44).","For ARB: mount and wire the compressor, run the air line to the locker, and test engagement before trail use."],"pros":["True locking traction on the trail eliminates the one-tire-spinning problem completely","ARB Air Locker delivers transparent on-road behavior \u2014 you'll forget it's there until you lock it","ELocker is a clean install without air lines and costs less than ARB","Significant traction upgrade for any JT trim without Rubicon's Tru-Lok system"],"cons":["Differential work requires precision \u2014 backlash and bearing preload errors cause gear noise and premature wear","ARB setup adds compressor cost and complexity; total cost exceeds $1,200 with compressor","Full locker in the rear changes on-road handling during parking maneuvers \u2014 turning radius increases when locked","Detroit Truetrac is not a full locker; it won't match the traction of ARB or ELocker in extreme situations"]},{"id":"jt-regear","title":"Re-Gearing the Gladiator JT \u2014 4.56 and 4.88 Ratios","vehicle":"gladiator","category":"drivetrain","type":"upgrade_path","difficulty":5,"time_hours":{"low":6,"high":12},"cost_usd":{"low":900,"high":2500},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","torque wrench","socket set","bearing press","dial indicator","inch-pound torque wrench","gear marking compound","pinion depth tool set"],"skills_required":["differential rebuild","precision measurement","gear pattern reading","bearing and seal installation"],"build_tiers":["moderate_trail","rock_crawler"],"year_compatibility":["2020-2024"],"tags":["regear","drivetrain","dana-44","gears"],"parts":[{"name":"Yukon Gear 4.88 Ring & Pinion for Dana 44 Front (JT)","vendor":"Yukon Gear & Axle","price_estimate_usd":249},{"name":"Yukon Gear 4.88 Ring & Pinion for Dana 44 Rear (JT)","vendor":"Yukon Gear & Axle","price_estimate_usd":249},{"name":"Yukon Master Install Kit for Dana 44 (bearings, seals, shims)","vendor":"Yukon Gear & Axle","price_estimate_usd":269},{"name":"Motive Gear 4.56 Ring & Pinion for Dana 44","vendor":"Motive Gear","price_estimate_usd":199}],"citations":[{"title":"Yukon Gear Dana 44 Ring and Pinion Install Guide","url":"https://www.yukongear.com/tech-info/install-instructions"},{"title":"Gear Ratio Selection Guide for Lifted Jeeps","url":"https://www.quadratec.com/c/journal/jeep-gear-ratio-guide"}],"alternatives":["jt-locker-rear"],"summary":"Stock JT gearing (3.73) was calibrated for factory tires \u2014 run 37s without regearing and you're dragging a heavy truck through every gear while your fuel economy craters and your transmission runs hot.","description":"The Gladiator JT's stock gear ratio is 3.73 on most trims. That ratio was engineered around the factory 32\"\u201333\" tire diameter. When you step up to 35s, the effective final drive ratio gets numerically lower \u2014 the engine has to pull a larger tire through each rotation, and it doesn't have the multiplication it needs to do that efficiently. At 37s, the problem is pronounced: slow acceleration, labored highway cruising, poor low-end pulling power, and a transmission that works harder in every gear.\n\nThe JT's longer 137\" wheelbase also means more rotational mass in the driveshafts, and carrying a truck bed and its payload adds total system weight. The impact of running tall tires without a regear is more pronounced on the JT than on the JL because of that extra mass \u2014 you feel it earlier and more consistently. For a JT running 35s, 4.56 gears restore the drivetrain math. For a JT on 37s, especially in rock-crawling or regular trail use with gear in the bed, 4.88 is the correct answer.\n\nThe Dana 44 front (non-Rubicon trims have Dana 30 fronts \u2014 this guide covers the Dana 44 found on Rubicon and Mojave) and Dana 44 rear both need to be re-geared simultaneously. Mismatched front and rear ratios will cause binding in 4WD. Yukon Gear's 4.88 ring and pinion for the Dana 44 ($249 each axle) is well-proven and widely installed on JT/JL builds; the Yukon master install kit ($269) bundles all the bearings, seals, and shims needed so you're not sourcing pieces separately. Budget for professional installation unless you have prior differential rebuild experience \u2014 this is not a beginner job, and a poorly shimmed pinion will destroy a new gear set in short order.","steps":["Drain both differentials completely.","Front differential: remove front driveshaft, disconnect axle shafts, and remove differential cover.","Remove and mark pinion flange nut (count and record the rotational torque before removal for reference on reassembly bearing preload).","Remove the pinion shaft, bearing races, and carrier assembly. Clean the housing.","Install the new ring gear on the carrier; torque ring gear bolts to spec.","Set pinion depth using the new shim stack and a depth tool \u2014 this is the most critical measurement. Use gear marking compound to verify contact pattern before final assembly.","Install new pinion bearings, set pinion preload to spec using an inch-pound torque wrench on the pinion flange nut.","Install the carrier, set backlash (.006\"\u2013.010\" for Dana 44), verify contact pattern again.","Reinstall bearing caps and torque to spec. Install new axle seals.","Repeat for the rear differential.","Fill both axles with 75W-140 synthetic gear oil. Add ARB/locker additive if running a limited-slip unit.","Drive gently for the first 500 miles during gear break-in; re-inspect fluid at 500 miles and replace."],"pros":["Restores factory powertrain feel after tire upsizing \u2014 acceleration, highway cruising, and fuel economy all improve","4.88 gears dramatically improve crawl ratio on rock trails with 37s","JT's longer wheelbase and payload capacity make regearing more impactful vs. JL \u2014 the math favors it","Gear swap pairs naturally with a locker install (same labor, same differential teardown)"],"cons":["Professional installation strongly recommended \u2014 incorrect pinion depth and preload ruin new gear sets","Both axles must be geared simultaneously to avoid 4WD binding \u2014 costs double vs. a single axle swap","Total cost including labor typically runs $1,500\u2013$2,500 for parts and shop time on both axles","Non-Rubicon JT Sport/Willys/Overland have a Dana 30 front \u2014 regearing a Dana 30 is a different (and more limited) gear selection than the Dana 44"]},{"id":"pentastar-3-6-oil-cooler-leak","title":"Pentastar 3.6L oil cooler / filter housing leak (TSB fix)","vehicle":"gladiator","category":"engine","subcategory":"oil system","type":"how_to","difficulty":3,"time_hours":{"low":2.0,"high":4.0},"cost_usd":{"low":80,"high":180},"tools_required":["3/8\" drive ratchet","10mm socket","13mm socket","torque wrench","drain pan","funnel","coolant catch container"],"skills_required":["basic engine work","coolant system knowledge","torque spec adherence"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2020-present"],"tags":["oil cooler","filter housing","3.6L pentastar","coolant","oil leak","TSB","dorman"],"parts":[{"name":"Metal oil filter housing assembly (replaces plastic factory unit)","vendor":"Dorman","price_estimate_usd":95},{"name":"Oil filter housing gasket/O-ring kit","vendor":"Mopar","price_estimate_usd":18},{"name":"Engine coolant (Mopar OAT or equivalent)","vendor":"Mopar","price_estimate_usd":22},{"name":"Engine oil (5W-20 full synthetic, 6 quarts)","vendor":"Mopar / Pennzoil / Mobil 1","price_estimate_usd":42}],"citations":[{"title":"NHTSA TSB database \u2014 Pentastar 3.6L oil cooler housing failures","url":"https://www.nhtsa.gov/vehicle/2020/JEEP/GLADIATOR/PU/AWD"},{"title":"Dorman 926-959 product page","url":"https://www.dormanproducts.com"}],"alternatives":[],"summary":"The Pentastar 3.6L's plastic oil filter housing develops cracks around 60,000\u2013100,000 miles. When it fails, you get oil in the coolant, coolant in the oil, or both. The fix is replacing the factory plastic housing with a metal aftermarket unit. This isn't a difficult job \u2014 it's a medium-complexity engine bay task.","description":"The Pentastar 3.6L \u2014 shared across the JK, JL, and JT \u2014 uses a plastic oil filter housing that also integrates the oil cooler. The housing is a single assembly that sits on the front of the engine block. Coolant runs through it to cool the oil; oil runs through it to the filter.\n\nThe plastic material degrades over time from heat cycling. Cracks typically develop around the gasket surfaces, at the coolant ports, and at the threads for the oil filter cap. When a crack appears, you can get oil mixing into the coolant (look for a brownish, milky sheen on the coolant reservoir cap), coolant mixing into the oil (look for a coffee-with-milk color on the dipstick), or external leaks pooling under the truck.\n\n**Catch this early.** Oil-coolant mixing is a fast path to bearing damage and a ruined engine if left unaddressed. If your coolant smells like oil or your dipstick shows discoloration, stop driving and diagnose.\n\n- External oil leak near the front of the engine, below the intake\n- Coolant reservoir showing a brownish tint or emulsified appearance\n- Dipstick oil with a milky or frothy appearance (coolant contamination)\n- Coolant loss without visible external leaks\n- White smoke from exhaust (coolant burning in cylinders \u2014 late-stage)\n\nBefore pulling the housing, confirm the housing is the source. A flashlight and a rag: wipe the area around the housing clean, run the engine to temp, then let it cool slightly and look for fresh seepage. The housing-to-block gasket and the coolant hose connections are the most common leak points.\n\nIf you have oil-coolant mixing confirmed, the housing replacement is mandatory. You'll also need to flush the cooling system and change the oil after the repair \u2014 the contaminated fluids need to come out completely.\n\nThe factory plastic housing can be replaced with an identical plastic unit (the leak may return) or with a metal aftermarket housing. The metal housing is the better repair. Dorman makes a direct-fit metal replacement that covers JK, JL, and JT applications; verify your year and engine application before ordering.\n\nThe Dorman unit (product number in the 926-series for this application) retains the factory oil filter thread spec and coolant port locations. It bolts in where the factory unit came out.","steps":["Allow the engine to cool completely \u2014 at least 2 hours after last operation. Do not work on a hot cooling system.","Drain the coolant from the lower radiator drain petcock into a catch pan. Mark the container \u2014 used coolant is toxic and needs proper disposal.","Drain the engine oil \u2014 place a drain pan under the oil drain plug, remove the plug, remove the oil filter cap from the housing to help the oil drain faster.","Disconnect the coolant hoses from the oil cooler housing (two hoses, clamps). Have rags ready \u2014 residual coolant will drain.","Remove the oil filter housing mounting bolts (typically 10mm). Note the number and location.","Lift the housing assembly free of the block. Inspect the block sealing surface \u2014 clean it with a rag if there's residue.","Install the new metal housing with a fresh gasket/O-ring kit. Apply no sealant unless the kit instructions specify it. Torque mounting bolts to spec (check the Dorman instructions \u2014 typically 18 ft-lb for the housing bolts).","Reconnect coolant hoses. Reinstall drain plug and new oil filter.","Fill engine oil (6 quarts 5W-20 full synthetic for the 3.6L).","Refill cooling system with fresh coolant. Burp the system: leave the reservoir cap loose, run the engine to temp, watch for air bubbles, then seal.","Check for leaks after first full heat cycle. Recheck coolant level when cold."],"pros":["Metal housing eliminates the material failure mode that caused the original crack","Direct-fit replacement \u2014 no modifications required","Resolves oil-coolant mixing before it causes downstream engine damage"],"cons":["Must flush and refill both oil and coolant \u2014 adds to total cost","If coolant contamination reached the oil passages significantly, inspect further for bearing damage","Aftermarket housing quality varies \u2014 buy from a reputable supplier and verify fitment before ordering"]},{"id":"jt-winch","title":"Winch for the Gladiator JT \u2014 10,000 lb Minimum","vehicle":"gladiator","category":"recovery","type":"part","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":1,"high":2},"cost_usd":{"low":349,"high":1200},"tools_required":["socket set","torque wrench","wire crimpers or connectors","electrical tape","multimeter"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching","basic electrical"],"build_tiers":["mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2020-2024"],"tags":["winch","recovery","synthetic-rope"],"parts":[{"name":"Warn VR EVO 10-S Winch (10,000 lb)","vendor":"Warn","price_estimate_usd":449},{"name":"Smittybilt X20 10,000 lb Winch with Synthetic Rope","vendor":"Smittybilt","price_estimate_usd":349},{"name":"Warn M10000-S Winch (10,000 lb, synthetic rope)","vendor":"Warn","price_estimate_usd":749},{"name":"Warn Zeon 10-S Winch (10,000 lb)","vendor":"Warn","price_estimate_usd":1099}],"citations":[{"title":"Warn VR EVO 10-S Product Page","url":"https://www.warn.com/warn-vr-evo-10-s-winch"},{"title":"Winch Sizing Guide \u2014 Off-Road Basics","url":"https://www.warn.com/learn/winch-size-guide"}],"alternatives":["jt-front-bumper"],"summary":"The JT Gladiator's GVWR sits between 5,400 and 6,200 lbs depending on trim and options \u2014 a 10,000 lb winch is the correct minimum, and 10K is where most of the best-value hardware lives.","description":"The standard winch sizing formula is 1.5x GVWR. For a JT at roughly 5,600 lbs loaded, that puts you at 8,400 lbs \u2014 and 10,000 lb winches cover that with margin for difficult pulls at steep angles or through mud where load multiplies. Going under-rated on a truck this heavy is a false economy. The 10K class is also where the market concentrates: best selection, most competitive pricing, most accessory ecosystem support.\n\nSynthetic rope over steel cable is the right call for any JT going on real trails. Synthetic rope is lighter, safer when it breaks (it drops rather than whipping), and won't develop the wire burrs that shred gloves. Steel cable has a cost and abrasion advantage in very high-frequency use environments like commercial recovery work, but for trail use, synthetic is safer for everyone nearby. Every winch in this tier is available with synthetic rope \u2014 don't settle for a steel cable version unless you're specifically replacing a winch that already has a drum-worn rope.\n\nThe Warn VR EVO 10-S ($449) is the best-value entry point from the most reliable name in winches. It's not as feature-rich as the Zeon, but it's a genuine Warn product with IP68 water resistance and a three-stage planetary gear train that handles sustained pull without overheating the way budget winches do. The Smittybilt X20 ($349) is legitimate for the price \u2014 it's the entry point for owners who need recovery capability now and will upgrade later. The Warn M10000-S ($749) is the right pick for regular trail use: better thermal management, faster line speed, and a more robust motor than the VR EVO. The Zeon 10-S ($1,099) is a professional-grade winch for owners who use it hard and often.\n\nMost JT aftermarket steel front bumpers include a winch mounting plate at the correct depth for a standard 10\" winch footprint. Confirm your bumper's winch compatibility and fairlead type (hawse vs. roller) before ordering a winch \u2014 synthetic rope requires a hawse fairlead; steel cable uses a roller fairlead.","steps":["Confirm the front bumper's winch mounting plate dimensions and fairlead type before ordering.","Mount the winch to the bumper plate using the supplied hardware. Torque to spec.","Thread the synthetic rope through the hawse fairlead and attach the hook.","Route the winch motor leads to the battery. Most installs use ring terminals bolted to the battery \u2014 positive to positive, negative to chassis ground.","Route the control cable to the cab or leave the wireless remote receiver in an accessible location behind the bumper.","Test the winch both in and out under no-load conditions before the first trail day.","Spool the rope evenly under tension \u2014 do this by attaching to a tree and pulling the JT forward slowly until the drum is full."],"pros":["10K rating covers the JT's GVWR with recovery margin","Synthetic rope is safer and lighter than steel cable","Bumper-mounted integration is clean and doesn't sacrifice hitch access","Wide accessory ecosystem: snatch blocks, dampeners, straps are universal across brands"],"cons":["Winch without a compatible bumper is a useless purchase \u2014 bumper must come first","Adds 65\u201395 lbs to the front end \u2014 affects steering feel and front suspension load","Hitch-mounted winch receivers exist and work, but a hitch mount can't pull you forward when you're facing the obstacle","Synthetic rope requires periodic inspection for fraying and UV degradation"]},{"id":"jt-steering-shaft-recall","title":"Gladiator steering shaft recall (2020\u20132022)","vehicle":"gladiator","category":"steering","subcategory":"steering shaft","type":"diagnostic","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":0.5,"high":2.0},"cost_usd":{"low":0,"high":0},"tools_required":["flashlight"],"skills_required":["visual inspection","recall verification"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2020-2022"],"tags":["steering shaft","recall","TSB","intermediate shaft","2020","2021","2022","safety","NHTSA"],"parts":[],"citations":[{"title":"NHTSA Recall 21V-368 \u2014 Jeep Gladiator Intermediate Steering Shaft","url":"https://www.nhtsa.gov/recalls"},{"title":"Stellantis recall center","url":"https://www.mopar.com/en-us/care/recalls.html"}],"alternatives":[],"summary":"Stellantis issued a recall covering early Gladiators (2020\u20132022) for an intermediate steering shaft that could fracture under stress, resulting in loss of steering control. This is a safety recall \u2014 verify your VIN and confirm the repair has been completed before driving the truck.","description":"The intermediate steering shaft connects the steering column to the steering gear. On affected vehicles, the shaft assembly could fracture at the coupler under load conditions \u2014 particularly aggressive steering inputs, off-road use, or high steering loads. A fractured intermediate shaft means partial or complete loss of steering control.\n\nStellantis issued Recall 21V-368 (and a related service bulletin) covering 2020\u20132021 Gladiators manufactured within a specific production window. The fix is dealer-performed replacement of the intermediate steering shaft with an updated part.\n\nNot all Gladiators in this model year range are affected \u2014 the recall covers a specific production date range. The only way to know if your truck is in scope is to check the NHTSA recall database by VIN.\n\nGo to nhtsa.gov/recalls and enter your 17-character VIN. The site will return any open recalls associated with your specific vehicle. If the steering shaft recall appears and shows \"remedy not yet received,\" contact a Stellantis dealer and schedule the recall repair. This is covered under the recall at no cost.\n\nIf the recall shows \"remedy completed,\" the dealer has already performed the shaft replacement on your vehicle. The work should be documented in your service history.\n\nIf you own an early JT and haven't verified recall status, here's what an affected or marginal intermediate shaft may feel like:\n\n- **Loose or clunky steering:** A notchy or clunky sensation when turning, particularly at slow speeds or parking maneuvers\n- **Play in the wheel:** Noticeable dead zone at center before steering response begins\n- **Click or clunk on full lock:** A sharp sound at or near full steering lock \u2014 can indicate coupler binding or wear\n- **Steering effort change:** Sudden increase in required steering effort, particularly under load\n\nNone of these symptoms is confirmation that the shaft is about to fail \u2014 other steering components (track bar, ball joints, tie rod ends) create similar symptoms. But if your truck is in the recall range and hasn't had the recall completed, these symptoms make the verification more urgent.\n\nA dealer-replaced intermediate shaft on a 2021\u20132022 JT is the updated production part. The replacement part is not the same component that was recalled \u2014 it's the corrected design.\n\nIf you've had the recall completed and still experience steering symptoms, those are coming from elsewhere: track bar, ball joints, tie rod ends, or alignment. The shaft replacement addresses the specific fracture failure mode, not all steering feel issues.","steps":["Locate your VIN (driver's door jamb sticker or dashboard through the windshield)","Go to nhtsa.gov/recalls and enter the VIN","Note whether the steering shaft recall appears and whether the remedy has been completed","If open: contact a Stellantis/Jeep dealer and schedule the recall repair","While waiting for the repair, drive carefully and avoid aggressive steering inputs at speed","After repair completion: verify the dealer recorded the completed recall in your service history"],"pros":["The recall repair is free \u2014 no cost to the owner","The replacement part resolves the specific fracture failure mode","Stellantis's recall process is accessible and straightforward"],"cons":["This is a safety recall; driving an unrepaired truck is a real risk","Dealer scheduling during recall campaigns can create wait times","Used-market JTs may have unknown recall status \u2014 always verify VIN before purchase"]},{"id":"jt-budget-lift","title":"Budget Lift Kit for the Gladiator (\u2264$600)","vehicle":"gladiator","category":"suspension","type":"kit","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":3,"high":5},"cost_usd":{"low":149,"high":599},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","socket set","torque wrench","pry bar"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching","wheel removal"],"build_tiers":["mild_trail","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2020-2024"],"tags":["lift","suspension","budget","spacer"],"parts":[{"name":"Rough Country 2\" Lift Kit for Gladiator JT","vendor":"Rough Country","price_estimate_usd":199},{"name":"Teraflex 1.5\" Spacer Lift Kit for Gladiator JT","vendor":"Teraflex","price_estimate_usd":299},{"name":"Daystar 2\" Coil Spacer Kit for JT","vendor":"Daystar","price_estimate_usd":149}],"citations":[{"title":"Gladiator JT Lift Kit Guide \u2014 Quadratec","url":"https://www.quadratec.com/c/journal/jeep-gladiator-lift-kit-buyers-guide"},{"title":"Teraflex JT Spacer Lift Installation","url":"https://www.teraflex.com/products/gladiator"}],"alternatives":["jt-mid-lift"],"summary":"A budget lift gets your Gladiator off stock ride height for a better stance and a little extra tire clearance \u2014 but know what you're paying for and what you're not.","description":"Under $600 buys you coil spacers or a basic coil replacement up front, and that's roughly it. The Gladiator JT runs a coil-sprung front \u2014 same architecture as the JL Wrangler \u2014 but uses leaf springs in the rear. That rear leaf spring setup is the key detail that separates the JT from the JL, and it's why most budget kits only address the front. A 2\" front spacer on a stock JT will leave the rear sitting lower than the front unless you add AAL (add-a-leaf) packs or swap to longer rear leaf springs. Some builders intentionally run a slight nose-up rake for clearance; most prefer level. Factor that into your budget before you order.\n\nThe Rough Country 2\" kit ($199) is the most affordable complete solution, including a basic rear block to address the rake issue. It won't transform the suspension geometry, but it will clear 33s without rubbing and give you the stance upgrade most Gladiator owners want in year one. The Teraflex 1.5\" spacer kit ($299) is a better-quality option that uses CNC-machined billet spacers and includes all hardware \u2014 it's the right call if you want something you won't replace in 18 months. Daystar's 2\" coil spacers ($149) are the barebones option: front spacers only, no rear component, intended for owners who just want a level look on pavement or light gravel.\n\nNone of these kits include geometry correction. That means no adjustable control arms, no alignment cam bolts, no caster correction. At 1.5\"\u20132\" of lift, the factory geometry is generally acceptable, but you will need a front-end alignment after install regardless. Budget $80\u2013$120 for the alignment shop visit \u2014 it's not optional. If you're planning to push to 35\" tires, a budget spacer kit is a reasonable starting point, but you'll want to add alignment cam bolts and potentially a track bar drop bracket to keep the handling honest.","steps":["Break the lug nuts loose on both front wheels before jacking up the vehicle.","Support the front axle under the differential with a floor jack, then raise and secure the vehicle on jack stands at the frame.","Remove the front wheels.","Unbolt the front sway bar end links \u2014 you'll need slack to lower the axle enough to install spacers.","Lower the jack slightly to decompress the coil springs. Install the coil spacers per the kit instructions, seating the spacer on top of the coil spring or on the upper mount depending on kit design.","Reinstall end links (use new hardware if provided), reinstall wheels, torque lug nuts to spec (100\u2013110 ft-lbs for JT).","For the rear (if your kit includes a rear component): place the axle on jack stands, remove the rear leaf spring U-bolts, insert the supplied block between the axle and leaf pack, reinstall and torque U-bolts to spec.","Lower the vehicle and get a front-end alignment before driving more than 20\u201330 miles."],"pros":["Lowest cost entry point for improved stance and minor tire clearance","Front install is beginner-friendly \u2014 no spring compressors required for spacer kits","Reversible \u2014 returns to stock by removing spacers","Clears 33\" tires on all JT trims with 2\" of lift"],"cons":["No geometry correction \u2014 alignment required, and handling degrades more noticeably above 2\"","Most kits do not fully address the rear leaf spring height \u2014 rake is common","Ride quality unchanged or slightly worse with spacers vs. replacement coils","Not a foundation for 35s or heavier wheeling; upgrading later means buying again"]},{"id":"jt-expedition-lift","title":"Expedition Lift for the Gladiator \u2014 Long-Arm System ($2,000+)","vehicle":"gladiator","category":"suspension","type":"kit","difficulty":5,"time_hours":{"low":20,"high":28},"cost_usd":{"low":2500,"high":5500},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","torque wrench","socket set","breaker bar","spring compressor","angle grinder","welder or MIG access","tape measure","alignment tool"],"skills_required":["suspension work","fabrication","torque spec adherence","driveline awareness"],"build_tiers":["moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2020-2024"],"tags":["lift","long-arm","suspension","expedition"],"parts":[{"name":"Rock Krawler 3.5\" Mid-Arm JT Lift Kit","vendor":"Rock Krawler","price_estimate_usd":3999},{"name":"EVO Mfg 4\" Long-Arm JT Lift Kit","vendor":"EVO Manufacturing","price_estimate_usd":4299},{"name":"Synergy Manufacturing 4\" JT Long-Arm Suspension System","vendor":"Synergy Manufacturing","price_estimate_usd":4499}],"citations":[{"title":"Rock Krawler JT Mid-Arm System Overview","url":"https://www.rockkrawler.com/gladiator-jt-suspension"},{"title":"EVO Mfg JT Long-Arm System \u2014 Install Manual","url":"https://evomfg.com/gladiator-jt-long-arm"}],"alternatives":["jt-mid-lift"],"summary":"A long-arm lift system transforms the Gladiator JT from a capable truck into a full-capability rig \u2014 but the JT's 137\" wheelbase and pickup platform introduce geometry considerations that don't exist on a Wrangler.","description":"The Gladiator JT has a 137\" wheelbase \u2014 19\" longer than the JL Unlimited. That extra length is the reason you can't simply bolt up a JL long-arm kit and call it done. Longer lower control arms change the pinion angle and caster geometry differently on the JT's longer frame. Quality JT-specific long-arm kits account for this with JT-tuned mounting brackets, appropriately lengthed arms, and driveshaft geometry calculations that reflect the JT's extended frame rails. Before ordering, confirm the kit is specifically engineered for JT \u2014 not a JL kit with \"JT compatible\" in the title.\n\nThe Rock Krawler 3.5\" Mid-Arm system ($3,999) is the most popular entry point for serious JT builds. \"Mid-arm\" here means longer-than-stock lower arms without relocating the frame mounting points \u2014 a meaningful upgrade in wheel travel and articulation without the full crossmember surgery of a true long-arm. EVO Mfg's 4\" Long-Arm ($4,299) does relocate the lower control arm mounts forward for maximum arm length, which delivers the best wheel travel and most linear suspension feel but requires more installation time and more care around the driveshafts. Synergy's 4\" system ($4,499) takes a similar approach with Synergy's adjustable arm design, which is the right call if you're planning future fine-tuning of the geometry.\n\nAt 3.5\"\u20134\" of lift with long arms, you're looking at 37\" tires as the natural fitment target. On Rubicon and Mojave trims, the Dana 44 fronts are already there. On Sport, Overland, and Willys trims with Dana 30 fronts, a 37\" setup is possible but the front axle becomes the weak link under hard use. Budget for a front locker and upgraded axle shafts if you're wheeling the Dana 30 on 37s regularly. Driveshaft upgrades are also worth pricing in advance \u2014 at 4\" of lift, the factory driveshaft U-joint angles push the limits of durability, especially on the rear.","steps":["Document all factory suspension geometry with measurements before disassembly: caster angle, pinion angle, front axle centering.","Raise the vehicle and support at the frame on jack stands; remove all four wheels.","Remove factory control arms (upper and lower), front and rear track bars, and front coil springs.","Install the new crossmembers or frame brackets per kit instructions \u2014 this is where most installs require drilling or welding depending on kit design.","Install new long lower control arms, then upper arms, adjusting length per kit specifications for your target lift height.","Install new front coil springs (supplied with kit), reconnect shocks.","Address the rear: install new leaf springs or replacement rear suspension per kit instructions; verify rear pinion angle.","Check driveshaft U-joint angles with a digital angle gauge \u2014 if front or rear exceeds 3\u00b0 static operating angle, budget for a driveshaft modification or replacement.","Reinstall wheels, torque all suspension fasteners to spec with wheels loaded (not hanging).","Schedule a 4-wheel alignment with a shop experienced in lifted Jeeps; specify arm lengths to the aligner."],"pros":["Maximum wheel travel and articulation for the JT platform","37\" tire fitment with no rubbing and proper geometry","Long-arm geometry dramatically reduces tire wear and improves on-road handling vs. stock-arm lifts at the same height","Foundation for a long-term build \u2014 you won't need to replace this suspension system"],"cons":["Highest cost and difficulty of any suspension option on the JT","Driveshaft modifications are likely at 4\" \u2014 budget an additional $400\u2013$800 per shaft","Requires a shop with JT long-arm alignment experience to set up correctly","JT-specific fitment must be verified \u2014 JL long-arm kits will not transfer directly","Installation is a full-weekend minimum; complex installs run two weekends"]},{"id":"factory-leaf-spring-axle-wrap","title":"Rear leaf spring axle wrap and the trailer-tow tradeoff","vehicle":"gladiator","category":"suspension","subcategory":"rear suspension","type":"diagnostic","difficulty":3,"time_hours":{"low":2.0,"high":5.0},"cost_usd":{"low":80,"high":600},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","breaker bar","3/8\" drive socket set","torque wrench"],"skills_required":["suspension work","jack stand safety","basic fabrication awareness"],"build_tiers":["mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2020-present"],"tags":["leaf spring","axle wrap","traction bar","anti-wrap","suspension","rear axle","hard acceleration"],"parts":[{"name":"Traction bar / anti-wrap bar (single or dual)","vendor":"Rancho / Rough Country / Synergy","price_estimate_usd":180},{"name":"Add-a-leaf pack (if using leaf re-arch approach)","vendor":"Deaver / Skyjacker / Old Man Emu","price_estimate_usd":220},{"name":"Rear shock upgrade (matched to ride height)","vendor":"Bilstein / Fox / King","price_estimate_usd":250}],"citations":[{"title":"Jeep Gladiator JT suspension engineering overview","url":"https://www.jeep.com/gladiator"}],"alternatives":[],"summary":"The Gladiator's leaf-spring rear is a truck feature, not a flaw \u2014 but it does create axle wrap under hard acceleration. Traction bars are the standard fix. If you're on a budget and not driving aggressively, re-arching the factory leaves first may be enough. If you're running 37s and an aggressive throttle, add a traction bar.","description":"Axle wrap happens when the leaf springs temporarily distort under the torque load of acceleration. In a leaf-spring setup, the spring pack is the only thing locating the axle fore-and-aft. When the drivetrain applies torque through the axle \u2014 especially under hard acceleration or when crawling in low range with high traction \u2014 the springs attempt to \"wind up.\" The axle moves forward at the pinion, the leaf pack flexes in an S-curve, and then snaps back.\n\nThe result ranges from a mild \"shudder\" felt at low speed under hard acceleration to a violent bucking on high-traction surfaces with larger tires. With the factory tire and power combination, most Gladiator owners don't push into axle wrap territory. Once you're running 35s or 37s and have any throttle aggression on trail, it becomes a real issue.\n\nSymptoms of axle wrap:\n- Shuddering or bucking sensation during hard acceleration from a stop\n- A \"clunk\" felt through the driveline when stabbing the throttle\n- Tire hop on high-traction surfaces (pavement, packed dirt) when accelerating hard\n- Visible spring pack distortion in photos or video \u2014 the S-curve shape is diagnostic\n\nAxle wrap is more pronounced with:\n- Larger, heavier tires (increased rotating mass)\n- Soft or fatigued spring packs (less resistance to wrap)\n- High-traction surfaces (asphalt, not sand or mud)\n- Aggressive throttle inputs\n\nA traction bar (also called an anti-wrap bar) adds a separate rigid link between the axle housing and the frame. It handles the fore-aft axle location that the spring pack was struggling with under torque. The leaf springs handle vertical load and some cornering; the traction bar handles acceleration torque.\n\nTraction bars for the JT typically mount to the frame near the factory spring perch and to the axle housing near the pinion side. Installation is a 2\u20134 hour job with a jack and basic tools. Most use existing mounting points; some require drilling or welding for optimal positioning.\n\n**Single vs. dual:** A single traction bar handles most trail and street applications. Dual traction bars (parallel setup) are for high-power builds or competition trucks that see aggressive axle loading regularly.\n\nBefore buying traction hardware, assess your spring pack. Factory leaf springs sag over time, losing arch and softening their resistance to wrap. A fatigued spring pack wraps more than a fresh one.\n\nNew-archleaf springs from a quality manufacturer (Deaver makes well-regarded Gladiator packs) restore the factory geometry and can improve wrap resistance without adding separate traction hardware. This is the budget-first approach for mild trail use without big tires. It won't match a traction bar on a lifted truck with 37s, but it may eliminate the symptom on a stock-height truck with 33s.\n\nThe JT runs leaf springs partly because Stellantis engineered it to tow. Leaf springs carry load better than a coil rear for most towing scenarios \u2014 they resist squat under tongue weight and maintain geometry better under sustained load.\n\nThe traction bar doesn't compromise towing capability. The two systems coexist without conflict. You can run a traction bar and still tow at the rated capacity.","steps":["Diagnose whether you actually have axle wrap (not just driveline clunk from worn U-joints or carrier bearing)","Inspect the spring pack for sag and fatigue \u2014 compare arch to factory spec","If spring pack is fatigued, consider re-arching first before adding traction hardware","If adding a traction bar: lift the vehicle on jack stands, measure the existing axle-to-frame relationship, select a traction bar with compatible mounting","Install per manufacturer instructions; torque all fasteners with vehicle at ride height (not lifted)","Test after installation: drive a high-traction surface under hard acceleration and verify the symptom is resolved"],"pros":["Traction bars are effective and affordable","They don't change ride quality significantly on street use","Compatible with towing use","Most are bolt-on installations"],"cons":["On a stock truck with stock tires, axle wrap may not be severe enough to justify the expense","Poor-quality traction bars can bind at full flex \u2014 verify the product is designed for JT geometry","Re-arching doesn't provide the same protection as a traction bar on lifted trucks with larger tires"]},{"id":"gladiator-vs-jl-mods-compatibility","title":"JT vs JL mod compatibility: what carries over, what doesn't","vehicle":"gladiator","category":"suspension","subcategory":"platform comparison","type":"reference","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":0.5,"high":1.0},"cost_usd":{"low":0,"high":0},"tools_required":[],"skills_required":["research"],"build_tiers":["mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2020-present"],"tags":["JL compatibility","lift kit","suspension","mod compatibility","leaf spring","coil spring","platform"],"parts":[],"citations":[{"title":"Jeep Gladiator JT Engineering \u2014 Stellantis","url":"https://www.media.stellantis.com"}],"alternatives":[],"summary":"The Gladiator shares its front end with the JL Wrangler \u2014 but the rear is a completely different platform. The JT is longer, leaf-sprung in the rear, and requires JT-specific lift kits for any rear suspension work. Buying a JL kit for your Gladiator will cost you money and leave you with parts that don't fit.","description":"Everything ahead of the firewall \u2014 the front suspension geometry, the Dana 44 front axle, the front coil springs, the front track bar, the front control arms \u2014 is shared between the JL Wrangler and the JT Gladiator. That's important for two reasons:\n\n**Good news:** JL front suspension parts cross-reference to the JT. JL lift coils, JL front track bars, JL upper and lower front control arms all fit the JT. The aftermarket JL component selection is huge, and most of it works on the Gladiator front end. If you're doing front-only suspension work, you have a massive parts pool.\n\n**Bad news:** Some \"JL lift kits\" sold as complete kits include rear components that don't fit the Gladiator. Verify that any \"complete\" kit is specifically listed as JT-compatible, not just JL-compatible. The manufacturer needs to have explicitly called out the JT.\n\nThe JL Wrangler runs coil springs in the rear. The Gladiator runs leaf springs. This is not a minor difference \u2014 it's a different suspension architecture with different geometry, different lift mechanics, and different failure modes.\n\n**What this means practically:**\n- JL rear lift coils do not fit the JT. The mounting architecture doesn't exist.\n- JL rear track bars, JL rear control arms, JL rear shocks (for a lifted JL) are calibrated for a different rear geometry. Some may physically fit in a modified application; none are engineered for it.\n- JT-specific rear lift kits use replacement leaf spring packs, add-a-leaf packs, or block-and-shackle combinations to achieve lift height.\n\nThe Gladiator runs a longer wheelbase than the JL (137 inches vs. 118 inches in the JL two-door). The driveshafts are longer, the rear proportions are different, and the leaf spring rear means different handling characteristics under load.\n\n| Component | JL \u2192 JT? |\n|---|---|\n| Front coil springs | Yes \u2014 same spec |\n| Front track bar | Yes \u2014 same geometry |\n| Front upper control arms | Yes |\n| Front lower control arms | Yes |\n| Front shocks | Verify by application \u2014 many cross |\n| Front bumpers, skid plates | Mostly yes \u2014 verify each part |\n| Rear coil springs | No \u2014 JT is leaf-sprung |\n| Rear track bar | No \u2014 JT uses different architecture |\n| Rear shocks | Some cross; verify JT application specifically |\n| Sway bar links | Verify \u2014 front may cross, rear different |\n| Rear leaf springs | JT-only \u2014 no JL equivalent |\n\nBuy kits explicitly listed as Gladiator JT or JT/JL. Kits listed as \"fits JK/JL/JT\" have been verified by the manufacturer for all three applications and are generally safe. Kits listed as \"fits JL/JL\" where the JT is mentioned as an afterthought in a footnote deserve more scrutiny.\n\nCompanies like Teraflex, Metalcloak, Skyjacker, Rough Country, and AEV all make JT-specific kits. AEV in particular has deep Gladiator content \u2014 they were involved with the JT platform early.\n\nThe driveshaft angle consideration: the JT's longer wheelbase means driveshaft angles change differently at a given lift height compared to the JL. Kits designed for the JT account for this. JL kits don't.","steps":["Identify whether the modification is front-only, rear-only, or a full suspension system","For front-only work, search for JL parts and verify JT cross-compatibility with the manufacturer","For rear work, search exclusively for JT-specific parts \u2014 do not assume JL rear parts cross over","For complete lift kits, confirm the kit explicitly covers the JT application (not just listed as \"compatible\")","Call the manufacturer's tech line if you're unsure \u2014 they know their fitment better than any catalog"],"pros":["The JL front end community is huge; JT owners benefit from that parts pool","JT-specific kits are well-developed from established manufacturers","The leaf-spring rear is actually advantageous for heavy towing and overland use"],"cons":["Incomplete kits sold as \"JL/JT compatible\" sometimes include JL-specific rear components that won't fit","The JT aftermarket is smaller than the JL \u2014 some parts require longer lead times","Driveshaft angles after lift need JT-specific consideration, not JL-borrowed specs"]},{"id":"jt-mid-lift","title":"Mid-Tier Lift for the Gladiator \u2014 2.5\"-3.5\" with Geometry Correction ($700\u2013$2,000)","vehicle":"gladiator","category":"suspension","type":"kit","difficulty":3,"time_hours":{"low":6,"high":10},"cost_usd":{"low":699,"high":2000},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","torque wrench","socket set","breaker bar","spring compressor","pry bar","alignment tool"],"skills_required":["suspension work","basic wrenching","torque spec adherence"],"build_tiers":["mild_trail","moderate_trail","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2020-2024"],"tags":["lift","suspension","geometry","leaf-spring"],"parts":[{"name":"EVO Mfg Enforcer 2.5\" JT Lift Kit","vendor":"EVO Manufacturing","price_estimate_usd":1299},{"name":"JKS Manufacturing 2.5\" JT Lift Kit","vendor":"JKS Manufacturing","price_estimate_usd":899},{"name":"Teraflex 2.5\" Performance Spacer Lift JT","vendor":"Teraflex","price_estimate_usd":699},{"name":"Mopar 2\" Lift Kit for Gladiator JT","vendor":"Mopar","price_estimate_usd":995}],"citations":[{"title":"EVO Mfg JT Enforcer Install Guide","url":"https://evomfg.com/gladiator-jt-suspension"},{"title":"JKS Gladiator JT Lift \u2014 Product Page","url":"https://jksmfg.com/jeep-gladiator"}],"alternatives":["jt-budget-lift","jt-expedition-lift"],"summary":"The 2.5\"\u20133.5\" range is where the Gladiator JT's suspension setup pays off \u2014 enough lift for 35s on any trim, enough height to address departure angle on the truck bed, and the geometry correction that separates a good lift from a frustrating one.","description":"At this price tier you're buying replacement coils up front, not spacers, plus control arm corrections and track bar adjustments that keep the front axle centered and the handling predictable on-road. The Teraflex 2.5\" Performance kit ($699) leads the value conversation \u2014 it's replacement coils with alignment cam bolts and an adjustable front track bar, which addresses most of the geometry shortcomings of a 2.5\" lift without going to full adjustable control arms. The JKS 2.5\" kit ($899) adds better shocks and a slightly more complete geometry package. EVO Mfg's Enforcer at $1,299 is the proper build: front and rear addressed together, quality control arms, and EVO's reputation for JL/JT fitment accuracy.\n\nThe Mopar 2\" kit ($995) deserves a mention specifically for owners who want to preserve their factory powertrain warranty. It's dealer-installed, FIAT/Chrysler-approved, and Mopar will stand behind it if anything goes sideways with warranty claims. It won't give you the wheel travel or geometry correction of the aftermarket kits, but the warranty peace-of-mind is real for newer trucks still under coverage.\n\nThe JT's rear leaf spring suspension is the ongoing compatibility headache with this platform. Every JL-based lift kit you find on the shelf was designed around the JL's rear coil springs. Some manufacturers sell JT-specific versions of their JL kits; others retrofit the front components and include JT-specific rear leaf spring packs or AAL kits separately. Before you order anything, confirm the listing says JT \u2014 not JL \u2014 and verify it includes a rear component. Running a leveled front with stock rear leaves on a truck that hauls gear will look wrong and handle worse than stock.","steps":["Break lug nuts loose on all four corners before lifting.","Raise the vehicle and support on jack stands at all four frame rails.","Remove all four wheels.","Front: disconnect sway bar end links, unbolt shock absorbers top and bottom, remove the front trackbar from the axle bracket.","Lower the front axle on the floor jack to decompress front coil springs. Remove and replace front coil springs per kit instructions.","Install adjustable front track bar and alignment cam bolts per kit instructions.","Reinstall front shocks (new shocks if included), reconnect sway bar end links, reinstall wheels.","Rear: support rear axle, unbolt rear shocks, loosen U-bolts on rear leaf springs.","Install rear leaf spring packs or AAL per kit instructions, reinstall U-bolts and torque to spec.","Reinstall rear shocks and wheels.","Torque all fasteners to spec \u2014 track bar bolts, control arm bolts, and U-bolts all have specific torque values; use the kit's torque sheet.","Schedule a 4-wheel alignment immediately after install."],"pros":["Clears 35\" tires on all JT trims with no rubbing","Geometry correction included \u2014 adjustable track bar and cam bolts prevent handling problems","Full kit approach addresses both front coil and rear leaf spring in one purchase","Mopar option preserves factory warranty coverage"],"cons":["Rear leaf spring compatibility requires careful verification before ordering \u2014 JL kits will not work","Higher cost and complexity vs. budget spacer kits","Professional alignment required after install ($80\u2013$150)","Aftermarket shocks in mid-tier kits are adequate but not exceptional \u2014 shock upgrade is a common follow-on purchase"]},{"id":"mojave-fox-shock-service","title":"Mojave-specific Fox 2.5 internal bypass shock service","vehicle":"gladiator","category":"suspension","subcategory":"shocks","type":"how_to","difficulty":4,"time_hours":{"low":4.0,"high":8.0},"cost_usd":{"low":200,"high":600},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","breaker bar","19mm socket","shock spring compressor (if coilover)","nitrogen fill equipment or shop access"],"skills_required":["suspension service","shock rebuild experience or shop delegation"],"build_tiers":["moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2020-present"],"tags":["mojave","fox","2.5 internal bypass","shock service","desert","suspension"],"parts":[{"name":"Fox 2.5 internal bypass shock rebuild service (per shock)","vendor":"Fox Racing Shox","price_estimate_usd":120},{"name":"Fox shock oil (FLOAT fluid, for bypass shock use)","vendor":"Fox Racing Shox","price_estimate_usd":28},{"name":"Nitrogen fill service (at a qualified shop)","vendor":"local suspension shop","price_estimate_usd":40}],"citations":[{"title":"Fox Racing Shox \u2014 service center locator","url":"https://www.ridefox.com/fox17/content.php?page=service-center"},{"title":"Jeep Gladiator Mojave product page","url":"https://www.jeep.com/gladiator"}],"alternatives":[],"summary":"The Mojave's Fox 2.5-inch internal bypass shocks are the most sophisticated suspension hardware on any factory Jeep. They need professional service every 30,000\u201350,000 miles or after any serious abuse. Don't skip this \u2014 a neglected bypass shock loses its calibration well before it shows visible damage.","description":"Standard shocks control damping through a single piston with calibrated valving. The Fox 2.5 Internal Bypass shock runs additional bypass tubes alongside the main shock body. As the shock compresses through its travel, the bypass tubes progressively restrict flow \u2014 the shock becomes firmer as it approaches full compression. This gives the Mojave an extremely supple ride at small suspension inputs (highway, light trail) while providing firm resistance at the end of travel (preventing bottoming on high-speed terrain).\n\nThis calibration is engineered specifically for the Mojave. The bypass positions (where the bypass tubes open and close) are set for the Mojave's ride height, travel, and the specific desert-running use case. A standard Fox 2.5 reservoir shock \u2014 even from the same diameter family \u2014 is not the same product.\n\nFox's general recommendation for performance shocks in trail use: inspect every 10,000\u201315,000 miles, service every 30,000\u201350,000 miles. For a Mojave used in actual desert running \u2014 Sonoran desert, Baja-style terrain, high-speed washboard \u2014 shorten that to 20,000\u201330,000 miles.\n\nSigns your shocks need service before the mileage interval:\n- Reduced damping feel \u2014 the truck feels \"floaty\" over terrain it used to manage firmly\n- Oil residue or weeping around the shock body or reservoir\n- Unusual noise (clunking, cavitation) from the shock during compression\n- Loss of the distinct firmness-at-end-of-travel characteristic\n\nFox recommends their factory-certified service centers for internal bypass shocks. The bypass tube positioning requires specialized knowledge \u2014 it's not a generic shock rebuild. Incorrect reassembly changes the shock's calibration profile, which defeats the point of having bypass shocks.\n\nLocate a Fox-certified service center via their website before shipping or bringing the shocks in. Some high-end off-road shops have bypass rebuild experience and appropriate equipment. Ask specifically if they've serviced Fox internal bypass shocks, not just Fox reservoir shocks \u2014 they're different processes.\n\n**What the service includes:** Disassembly, inspection of seals, piston, bypass tubes, and body; cleaning; replacement of seals and oil; nitrogen recharge to spec; recalibration of bypass positions; reassembly and testing.\n\nThe Mojave's shocks are calibrated for speed and compliance over large undulations \u2014 desert washes, high-speed dirt roads, Baja-style terrain. The valving and bypass positions favor this use case.\n\nThis calibration is not ideal for slow-speed rock crawling. At low speed, the bypass tubes are mostly open, which means the shock is relatively underdamped for technical rock terrain. The Mojave is genuinely better in the desert than on a technical rock trail \u2014 that's by design.\n\nIf you primarily run rocks and bought a Mojave, that's workable \u2014 it's not undriveable on rocks \u2014 but understand that the suspension is optimized for a different environment.\n\n**DIY:** Removing and reinstalling the shocks is within reach for someone comfortable with suspension work. It requires removing the wheels, unbolting the shock ends, and dealing with the coilover spring if applicable. The spring compressor is mandatory.\n\n**Send out:** The rebuild itself is shop work for most people. Sending the shocks to Fox or a certified rebuilder and reinstalling the rebuilt units is the practical approach for anyone who doesn't have bypass-specific rebuild experience.","steps":["Inspect shocks at each oil change \u2014 look for weeping and check damping feel","At 30,000 miles or when performance drops, schedule service","Locate a Fox-certified service center or qualified bypass shock rebuilder","Remove shocks from the vehicle (this is DIY-doable; see your vehicle-specific removal procedure)","Ship or bring shocks to the service center with your weight/application info","Reinstall rebuilt shocks; torque to spec with truck at ride height"],"pros":["The Fox 2.5 internal bypass is genuinely superior suspension hardware for desert running","Proper service restores calibration \u2014 the shock performs like new after a rebuild","Fox's service center network is well-established"],"cons":["Service cost per shock is significant \u2014 plan $400\u2013$600 for a full set rebuild","Finding a qualified local rebuilder may require shipping the shocks out","The desert calibration is a real limitation for technical rock use"]},{"id":"jt-tire-fitment-37s","title":"37s on a Gladiator: what it really takes","vehicle":"gladiator","category":"wheels_tires","subcategory":"tire fitment","type":"upgrade_path","difficulty":4,"time_hours":{"low":8.0,"high":20.0},"cost_usd":{"low":3500,"high":8000},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","breaker bar","torque wrench","angle grinder (for fender trimming)"],"skills_required":["tire mounting","gear oil change","alignment basics","fender modification awareness"],"build_tiers":["moderate_trail","rock_crawler"],"year_compatibility":["2020-present"],"tags":["37 inch tires","tire fitment","gear ratio","4.88","rubicon","fender trim","lift kit","payload"],"parts":[{"name":"37-inch tire (e.g., 315/70R17 or 37x12.5R17)","vendor":"BFGoodrich / Falken / Mickey Thompson / Nitto","price_estimate_usd":280},{"name":"Re-gear ring and pinion \u2014 front Dana 44 (4.88 ratio)","vendor":"Yukon Gear / Motive Gear / Nitro Gear","price_estimate_usd":320},{"name":"Re-gear ring and pinion \u2014 rear Dana 44 (4.88 ratio)","vendor":"Yukon Gear / Motive Gear / Nitro Gear","price_estimate_usd":320},{"name":"JT-specific lift kit (2.5\" minimum for 35s; 3.5\" for 37s)","vendor":"Teraflex / Metalcloak / Skyjacker","price_estimate_usd":800},{"name":"Differential bearing kit (front and rear, for re-gear)","vendor":"Timken / Motive Gear","price_estimate_usd":120}],"citations":[{"title":"Gladiator JT Rubicon factory spec \u2014 Jeep Media","url":"https://www.media.stellantis.com"}],"alternatives":[],"summary":"A Rubicon leaves the factory on 33-inch tires with 4.10 gears. You can bolt 35s on with a 2.5-inch lift. 37s require gearing, fender modification, possibly brake upgrades, and a hard look at the payload math \u2014 the Rubicon's already tight at 1,200 lb. Do this upgrade with your eyes open.","description":"Rubicon: 33-inch LT285/70R17 mud-terrains, 4.10 axle gears, Rock-Trac 4:1 transfer case.\nSport/Willys: 32-inch LT255/75R17 all-terrains, 3.73 axle gears.\nMojave: 33-inch Falken Wildpeaks, 3.73 axle gears.\n\nThe Rubicon's 4.10 gears are designed for 33-inch tires. The relationship between tire size and gear ratio determines how hard the engine works at highway speed and how much torque multiplication you have in the lower ranges. Going to 35s on 4.10s is noticeable but livable. Going to 37s on 4.10s will strain the engine at highway speed, kill fuel economy, and put stress on the transmission.\n\nWith a 2.5-inch lift, 35-inch tires (315/60R17 or 35x11.5R17) fit a Gladiator without fender trimming. Most JT-specific 2.5-inch lift kits include the right hardware to clear this size. Stick with a proper JT-specific kit \u2014 the rear is leaf-sprung and requires JT-specific components, not JL parts.\n\nThe 4.10 gears on a Rubicon are borderline acceptable with 35s. If you highway-drive at freeway speeds regularly, budget for a re-gear to 4.56 or 4.88 when you put on 35s. It's not catastrophic without it, but the truck performs better with appropriate gearing.\n\n37-inch tires change the build equation in several ways simultaneously:\n\n**Gearing (mandatory):** 37s on 4.10 gears will make the Gladiator sluggish on highway, strain the drivetrain, and reduce fuel economy significantly. 4.56 is the minimum; 4.88 is better. The re-gear means pulling both front and rear differentials, installing new ring and pinion sets, and setting up lash and backlash correctly. This is a shop job unless you have a ring-and-pinion setup background.\n\n**Lift:** 37s need 3\u20133.5 inches of lift for clearance. This moves the driveshaft angles beyond the factory range and requires a full-length JT-specific kit with control arm corrections. Long-arm kits improve the geometry further at this lift height.\n\n**Fender trimming:** The JT Rubicon has plastic fender flares that extend 4 inches from the body. At full articulation with 37s, even with a 3-inch lift, the tire can contact the fender flare. The fix is trimming the inner fender well liner and possibly trimming the flare itself. This is a cut-and-not-uncut decision \u2014 verify fitment at full stuff before you cut.\n\n**Brake consideration:** The factory brake system is sized for 33-inch tires. Going to 37s means more rotating mass and a longer lever arm to stop. The factory brakes can stop a stock truck on 37s, but stopping performance degrades. High-performance brake pads are a worthwhile addition before you start running 37s on trail.\n\n**Payload impact:** The Rubicon already runs 1,200 lb payload. 37-inch tires weigh approximately 15 lb more each than 33s \u2014 that's 60 lb added to the unsprung rotating weight. Every pound of tire and wheel added reduces your available payload. On a Rubicon loaded for trail with passengers and gear, this math gets tight quickly.","steps":["Confirm current axle ratio (door sticker or manual)","Price a re-gear: 4.56 for 35s, 4.88 for 37s \u2014 get quotes from two shops","Select a JT-specific lift kit for the height required (not a JL kit)","Mount one tire test-fitment before buying the full set \u2014 verify clearance at full lock and full articulation","Trim fender liner/flare as needed \u2014 minimum cuts first, test fitment after each cut","After re-gear: run break-in procedure per the gear oil manufacturer's instructions (typically avoid extended load for first 500 miles)","Get an alignment after lift installation"],"pros":["37s dramatically increase trail capability on the JT's already-solid platform","The Dana 44s handle 37s without heroic axle upgrades (unlike XJ 8.25 builds)","The aftermarket has well-developed JT-specific 37-inch fitment solutions"],"cons":["Full cost including re-gear, lift, tires, and alignment approaches $5,000\u2013$8,000","Re-gearing the front and rear is shop-level work for most owners","Rubicon payload tightens further with the additional tire/wheel weight","Fender trimming is irreversible \u2014 measure twice"]}];